==============================================================================================================================
u02 -- ANGLE A: a SECOND acceleration scale a_1.  One free parameter against the whole liability ledger,
      then the same a_1 against the galactic successes that survived.
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] 0a the per-row inversions 'what a_1 would this row alone require' are exact for every mechanism, over y = 1e-4..50 and required boosts 0.1x to 45x -- so a spread in the required a_1 across rows is a real disagreement between rows and not an artefact of the solver   (M0: 1.68e-13, M1: 2.22e-16, M2: 1.44e-15, M6: 3.33e-16)
  [PASS] 0b STRUCTURAL, and it decides half the answer before a single number is fitted: M1, M2, M3, M4 and M7 are boosts, so they produce R >= 1 at every y and for every a_1.  A row that needs R < 1 -- a system the framework OVER-predicts -- is unreachable by ANY of them at ANY a_1.  Only M6 (a deep return to Newton) can go below 1, and it is bounded from below by 1/nu(y), i.e. it can at most undo the kernel and never go below the Newtonian prediction   (at y = 0.05, nu = 4.99: M6's floor is R = 1/nu = 0.2004, i.e. the most a deep return to Newton can remove is 0.698 dex)

  ledger: 37 rows -- 35 AMPLITUDE misses that a boost could in principle address, plus the HI warp onset (a LOCATION failure) and the Fundamental Plane tilt (a GRADIENT failure), which are carried but never fitted.
  the alt-footing y is the canonical y scaled by a_0(can)/a_0(alt) = 0.8283; the alt R comes from the source .out's own alt row, not from a rescaling.
  [PASS] L2 (can fail) the ledger is not a transcription taken on trust: eight rows spanning all three source reductions are re-read out of the committed .out files themselves and must match the numbers used below   (8 spot rows verified against their .out)
  canonical   rows needing MORE boost (R>1): 26    rows needing LESS boost (R<1):  9    span of R: 0.070 to 44.7
  alt         rows needing MORE boost (R>1): 26    rows needing LESS boost (R<1):  9    span of R: 0.065 to 40.9
  [PASS] 1a (can fail, and it is the single most consequential number in this analysis) a SECOND BOOST CANNOT BE THE ANSWER BY COUNTING ALONE: 9 of the 35 amplitude liabilities need the framework to predict LESS, not more. M1, M2, M3, M4 and M7 raise the prediction at every acceleration and for every a_1, so those 9 rows are outside the reach of any second boost whatsoever -- before any fit is attempted, a second scale can address at most 26/35   (R<1 rows: NGC1052-DF4 (-1.155 dex), Pal 4 (globular) (-0.781 dex), Pal 14 (globular) (-0.658 dex), NGC1052-DF2 (-0.485 dex), Tidal dwarf galaxies (-0.394 dex), NGC 2419 (globular) (-0.199 dex), Milky Way K_z(1.1 kpc) (-0.115 dex), LG field dwarfs (EFE-free) (-0.088 dex), Pal 3 (globular) (-0.075 dex))
  [PASS] 1b (can fail) and the eight are not off in a corner of the acceleration axis where a second scale could simply not reach them: inside the SINGLE decade 0.005 < g_bar/a_0 < 0.1 the ledger contains rows of BOTH signs. Any R(y) is a function of y alone, so it takes one value there and cannot be both above and below 1   (15 rows in that decade, R from 0.070 (NGC1052-DF4) to 15.67 (Coma UDGs))

